WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.193
- [Narrator] The Supreme Court

00:00:01.193 --> 00:00:03.870
has a lot of controversial
cases on its docket,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.600
whether domestic abusers
should have access to guns,

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.790
whether the Consumer
Financial Protection Bureau

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.510
is constitutional, and if the
abortion pill should be legal.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.720
All of these cases come
from one lower court,

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.180
the same one that also
sent the court big cases

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.240
about the Affordable
Care Act and abortion.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.060
It's the US court of appeals
for the Fifth Circuit,

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.450
arguably the most conservative
appeals court in the country.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.360
And strangely, even though
they're bringing a conservative

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.760
Supreme Court conservative cases,

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.360
their most pressing rulings
are being overturned a lot.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.520
- You have case after case

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.750
where the Fifth Circuit had
done something that was too far

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.230
to the right or too procedurally extreme

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.090
even for this Supreme Court.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.140
- [Narrator] That's Stephen Vladeck,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.970
a professor and legal scholar.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.150
- The Federal appeals courts
are perhaps the most powerful

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.670
institution that we spend
the least time talking about

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.080
in our public discourse,

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.250
- [Narrator] And he'll help us explain

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.680
how the Fifth Circuit
became the go-to place

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.120
for controversial cases

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.913
and why so many are making
it to the Supreme Court.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.350
There are 12 appeals courts
representing different parts

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.090
of the country, and their judges,

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.400
just like Supreme Court
justices, are nominated

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.190
by the President and
confirmed by the Senate.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.290
That used to involve getting 60 votes

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.850
and the approval of the senators

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.620
from the state they'd be representing.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.020
So there was some bipartisanship needed.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.370
- You had really this long
period of time where most,

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.370
not all, but most of the
federal judges being confirmed

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.350
by the Senate were, you know,

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.030
maybe a little right of center,

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.240
a little left of center,
but pretty centrist.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.040
- [Narrator] Today, not so much.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.720
For example, President Trump was able

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.880
to appoint several judges
to the Fifth Circuit

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.200
who are different.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.670
- The judges are, I think,

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:51.540
on anyone's placement
pretty far to the right.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.130
- [Narrator] That's
because the process shifted

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.110
during the Trump administration.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.960
2017, the Senate changed
the rules so that nominees

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.800
only needed 50 or 51 votes to be confirmed

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.040
and no longer cared about the
home state senator's approval.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.610
So judicial nominees no longer
needed bipartisan support.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.440
- You're seeing the sort of the norm

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.470
for judicial confirmation be, you know,

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:18.210
who is the most
ideologically extreme person

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.490
I can get through the Senate today.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:23.220
For obvious reasons that
changes the calculus

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.860
for what kind of person
you're looking for.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.450
- [Narrator] So Trump nominated judges

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:28.890
and justices who had.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:31.770
- Extensive and controversial records.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.480
- [Narrator] Unlike previous
appeals court judges

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.970
who typically had backgrounds
in private practice

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.860
or as judges in other courts,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.440
most of Trump's judges, at
least in the Fifth Circuit,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.570
also had political backgrounds

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.970
either working for
campaigns, the White House,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.280
or directly for the Governor
and Attorney General

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.590
they now hear cases from.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.170
- I actually think that many of the judges

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.960
that we're seeing nominated
during the Trump administration

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.240
and confirmed during the
Trump administration,

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.040
and indeed many of the
judges we're seeing nominated

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.500
during the Biden administration
and confirmed today,

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.970
would never have been nominated

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.430
during prior presidencies
of the same party.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.530
And you know, I think folks
will have different views about

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.720
whether that's a good
thing or a bad thing.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.570
What I think we can't deny is it's a thing

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:16.260
and it's having a pretty
profound effect on the nature

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.580
of the federal judiciary.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.320
- [Narrator] But the
judges alone can explain

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.670
the controversial cases.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.080
They don't make them up.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.550
- Part of it is litigants
steering cases to these judges.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:30.180
The modus operandi is to file in Texas

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.460
where you can find a
sympathetic district judge

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.930
and where you know that any appeal

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.460
is going to the Fifth Circuit.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:36.840
- [Narrator] The case
against the abortion pill

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.300
was filed in Amarillo, Texas

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.830
because the district judge there,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.500
the court that hears the cases
before the appeals court does

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.180
is very conservative.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.090
Dobbs was brought in Mississippi

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.760
and the Fifth Circuit judges
can even make the system work

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.380
in their interest.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.090
When a case comes to the court of appeals,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.940
it's assigned to a panel
of three random judges.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:57.840
At the Fifth Circuit,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.820
the odds are pretty good
that a case will be heard

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.010
by a majority of conservative judges.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:05.130
But sometimes a case gets a
majority of liberal judges,

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.930
like with a case involving whether felons

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.060
should have the right
to vote in Mississippi.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.650
The panel ruled they should,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.440
but the full court can
decide to rehear cases

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.230
by the entire court what's called en banc,

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.530
which it plans to do for
that felons one in January.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:21.900
- Folks tend to use the
term judicial activism

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.020
as a shorthand for decisions
they disagree with,

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:27.570
and I think that in that
respect, the term is unhelpful.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.403
There is, however, I think
something that is especially

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:35.130
aggressive about the way the
Fifth Circuit's behaving,

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.340
not just in the substance of the decisions

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.780
that the court's handing down,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.540
but also just in its procedural behavior.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.450
So one great example of
this was the challenges

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.120
to the Biden administration's vaccination

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.940
or testing mandate for large employers.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.980
- [Narrator] Immediately,
dozens of nearly identical

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.720
lawsuits challenging the rule were filed

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:55.890
in different states.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.110
So many that every circuit
court in the country

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:59.790
would've heard the same case.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.800
It triggered a literal lottery.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.690
Each court with a pending
case would have a ball

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:06.360
put into a wooden drum and
whoever had theirs picked out

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.500
would hear the case.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.180
But before that lottery happened,

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.360
the Fifth Circuit cut ahead
and ruled on the case anyway,

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.850
blocking the Biden
administration's mandate.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.250
After the lottery, the
winning court reversed it

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.710
and allowed the policy to go into effect.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:23.580
- It's not just that you have
judges all the way to one side

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.210
of the ideological spectrum
who are being aggressive

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:32.070
in adopting and pushing
for new interpretations

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.860
of what had been pretty
settled legal concepts.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.830
It's that they're taking
procedural shortcuts,

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.950
and I think that's part of
why we're seeing increasingly

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.460
a disconnect between
the conservative judges

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:45.600
on the Fifth Circuit

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.150
and the conservative justices
on the Supreme Court,

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.210
you know, some of whom I
think are much more particular

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.790
about those procedural formalities.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.320
- [Narrator] President
Trump was able to appoint

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.660
a large number of polarizing judges.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.760
President Biden has done some of the same

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.910
thanks to that 50 vote
rule, but not as polarizing

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:05.250
as the Democratic Senate has mostly taken

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.440
the home state approval rule back up.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.960
- I think the real problem
at the end of the day

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.170
is you might be happy
with individual results

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.030
and individual cases,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.510
but our legal system is
not set up for a world

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:22.458
in which you can have
this kind of radical polls

00:06:22.458 --> 00:06:23.760
with no middle.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.970
What that really is a recipe for is chaos

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:31.800
and not a set of coherent,
uniform, legal principles

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.710
that actually might allow
us to function as one nation

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.110
as opposed to 50 different states.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:38.100
- [Narrator] With the Fifth Circuit

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.840
filled with conservative judges,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.380
it will continue to attract
the most conservative cases,

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.713
some of which will inevitably
end up at the Supreme Court.

00:06:46.713 --> 00:06:49.546
(dramatic music)

